this is a production of cornell  university  okay so i'm going to be talking about  our our diploid and triploid cultivar  trials that we've been doing these past  two years if you were here last year  then you got to see  year one in the field and then this is  our our year two trial in these first  two rows uh here  so let's just kind of go over some  basics what is the difference between a  diploid  hemp cultivar and a triploid hemp  cultivar well it's the number of sets of  chromosomes that the plant has so so  most hemp just like humans has two sets  of chromosomes one that they get from  their pollen parent from their dad and  one that they get from their seed parent  from their mom but there's kind of some  some fancy  chemical manipulation that we can do  that we can  make  a different number of chromosomes in the  plant  so this is something that oregon cbd did  if you look at the handout which you can  find on the qr code on  the agenda for the day  you can see that what they did is they  they treated some of their individuals  with a chemical called colchicine which  doubles the number of chromosomes  so instead of being a diploid two sets  of chromosomes they become a tetraploid  four sets of chromosomes and then when  you cross a tetraploid 4x with a diploid  2x you get a triploid 3x  does that make sense to everyone you're  tracking perfectly great uh so so the  next question why would we even want  triploids like what is is the value of  triploids and i'm going to give you a  hint uh things like seedless watermelon  and bananas are also triploid  naturally or man-made and the benefit in  some species of being triploid is that  they do not usually produce viable seeds  so when seeds are produced in the plant  the plant needs to divide up the  chromosomes into different seeds that  then get pollinated by pollen but you  can imagine that dividing three sets of  chromosomes does not go as well as  dividing two or four because three  doesn't divide evenly  so it's much harder for these plants to  make seed and often the triploids are  seedless are sterile  so people were interested in this from a  hemp perspective because if you're  producing cannabinoids you want  unpollinated female inflorescences so  pollen management is something that a  lot of growers have to deal with  something that drives people to do  indoor cultivation  to really need to to manage the  production of  males in their field and also  grain and fiber production in a several  mile radius because hemp and cannabis is  wind pollinated and that pollen can  travel really great distances  so the idea was that if we could have  these triploid cultivars they would be  seedless you could grow them without the  fear of pollination and to test that us  along with our colleagues at north  carolina state and the university of  kentucky decided to do a trial where we  planted these diploid and triploid  cultivars at sites that were pollen free  and also sites that were pollen  challenged so this is our pollen  challenge site you can see our grain and  fiber planting right behind it there's a  ton of pollen there so if these plants  were going to get pollinated we're  giving them the the best opportunity to  get pollinated here  so we're interested in comparing the  diploid and triploid cultivars at our  pollinated and pollen free sites and if  you look on our handout  i'm presenting some of the data from our  sites last year  really really exciting and interesting  data  so the diploids produce seed as expected  interestingly the triploids also  produced some seed dramatically less  seed but some seed still  so as a proportion of the total biomass  our diploids produced you know 40 or 50  percent of the total biomass with seed  whereas our triploids it was generally  less than 10 percent as low as one  percent even in a pollinated environment  so a pretty dramatic reduction in in  seed and that also corresponds with um  the the cannabinoid measurements so we  measured cannabinoids at three weeks  after the initiation of flowering five  weeks after the initiation of flowering  it also bulk stripped floral biomass and  for our diploid varieties we saw  significant reductions in cannabinoid  content at our pollinated sites so more  seed less cannabinoids probably a  combination of things like the the total  biomass being diluted by seed biomass  which is very low in cannabinoids  because the cannabinoids are not  produced in the seeds but if we look at  our triploids at our pollinated and  pollen free environments they produced  about the same amount of cannabinoids so  we didn't see that dilution effect even  though there was a little bit of seed  production  so  i think that's all i really want to say  you can see more on the handout and i  would be happy to take any questions or  we can walk around for a minute or two  before we have to go to luis and jamie's  talk right across the street here larry  has a question why are those plants so  small oh that's a great question larry  the the question from larry is why are  those plants so small if you were here  last year you may remember that the  plants were much bigger last year and  this was because  um  so these are these are transplanted hemp  plants so we grew them in the greenhouse  and you know you grow them in the  greenhouse with the anticipation that  your field is going to be ready when the  plants are ready in the greenhouse  unfortunately we had a very wet june and  this field did not have plastic it was  not plowed up we couldn't get the  tractors in here so we weren't able to  transplant that initial set of plants  that would have been you know probably  this big by now so what we did is we  planted a second round of seedlings  which were delayed about a month and  these are those so they're significantly  smaller than than one would expect but  we should be able to get still good data  off of them uh even though the plants  are smaller  any other questions yes tyler yeah i'm  just curious are all of these plants  triploids out here that we're going to  look at or do you have some comparison  like anyway  that's a great great question tyler um  so tyler asks if all of the plants are  diploi or all the plants are triploid or  if there's there's mixtures so the  diploid triploid trial is actually just  a very small portion of this field we  have a lot of other exciting stuff that  i don't get to talk about in the rest of  the field so these first two rows are  the diploid triploid trial and we have  three uh pairs of diploid and triploid  cultivars so three diploids and three  triploids and they're replicated in five  plant plots four times so there are both  diploids and triploids in the field yes  um  any other questions yes  what um what's the effect of the triplet  on the vegetative growth or other  aspects of the plant growth that's a  great question so the question was  does the being triploid compare to being  diploid  increase maybe the biomass or the  concentration of cannabinoids  so based on the data from last year i  don't think we saw evidence of that um  you know  in the in the pollinated trials  obviously the diploids produced seed and  the triploids did not um so there was a  difference in concentration there but  overall i don't think we see an effect  of triploids being you know larger than  diploids or producing 50 more  cannabinoids in an unpollinated field  yes tyler  have you worked at all you've mentioned  tetraploids as well have you tried to  grow out any tetraplasty  so tyler's asking if we have grown any  tetraploids here um and to my knowledge  we have not grown any tetraploids here  that would be really interesting to do  but our our lab has not gotten into the  whole  culture scene chromosome doubling deal  we're mostly relying on other uh people  who have done that work  and we're just evaluating those  already um so we got  it is a good question though  uh whether triploidy and fiberhemp for  example  might increase the yield  you would have to create it as a f1  hybrid obviously because it wouldn't  make any seed but you have to plant it  as a clone then no  no you you would cross a diploid with a  tetraploid  uh and that would create the triploid  seed and then you just take that seed  and plant that seed but  obviously those plants are then sterile  so you have to do that hybrid cross  every year to make that seed  potentially  yeah so that's a great point that  larry's making about there could be  applications for triploids in other  market classes of hemp including  fiberhemp  depending on what the effect of the  extra set of chromosomes does on the  other traits in the plants and the  possible benefit for fiber would be what  often triploids show increased yield  they're bigger than diploids  so we've done a lot of work on willow  most of our commercial willow cultivars  which are for biomass production are  triploids  is there any work on hybrid bigger with  him  uh so the question was is there any work  on hybrid vigor for hemp so just a  background for those of you unfamiliar  with hybrid vigor  in other crops the canonical system used  to make hybrids as you generate two  inbred lines i'll just use corn as an  example so you can  take two  uh  very different corn lines you can self  them until they're very inbred and then  you can use them to make a hybrid and  then that hybrid shows hybrid vigor or  heterosis so the progeny performed much  better than both of the parents  so this is much more challenging to do  in hemp because hemp is generally out  crossing if it's dioecious it's an  obligate outcrosser so it's much harder  to make inbred lines  there is some work on it from groups  like oregon cbd also our  group published a paper  last year looking at um different hybrid  families of hemp  all across to a single common parent and  how those different hybrids performed  so there definitely is  uh work on it but it is more challenging  in a diocese species than a normal  selfing species  yep are you noticing any differences in  the flower structure when you increase  poisoning  uh so the question was are there any  differences in flower structure with an  increase in ploydy  i  personally have have not noticed that  but that's also anecdotal i don't know  if people have um done  you know inflorescence architecture  quantification and done proper  statistics to test that hypothesis but  it would be definitely an interesting  hypothesis to  test they do flower later oh right  um so so in these these diploid triploid  comparisons we do see that the triploids  generally flower later um which could be  an effect of being triploid it also  could be effects of flowering time genes  that are segregating in these  populations so when oregon cbd makes  their  their hybrids they use an autoflower  parent as their pollen parent so they  can  well for a variety of reasons but um  that is uh one of the the things that is  just innately part of all of their  hybrids um  so in in diploids um the hybrids have  one dose out of two from that auto  flower parent but in  triploids it's one dose out of three so  if this flowering time is is dosage  dependent it could be an effect of the  the genes in addition to the ploidy  alrighty i think we're running out of  time here but um  um i'll be around if anybody has any  questions and i'm excited to hear what  luis and jamie have to talk about at the  next stop  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu

